<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Dulafid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">825 CE – 897 CE (approx.) · capital Karaj</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Arab family of governors who ruled the Jibal region of western Iran for most of the 9th century from their own city, Karaj.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-dulaf">Abu Dulaf al-Qasim ibn Isa</a>, of the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-ijl">Banu Ijl</a>, was a general and poet in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>. Around 825 he founded or developed the town of Karaj (Karaj Abi Dulaf, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arak-iran">Arak</a>, not the city near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>) and governed much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> as an Abbasid official.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Dulafids ruled hereditarily but in the name of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, collecting taxes in a rich agricultural and horse-raising region on the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-dulaf">Abu Dulaf</a> was celebrated as a patron and poet and appears in Arabic literature for his generosity. His descendants, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-abd-al-aziz-ibn-abi-dulaf">Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz</a>, fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> and sometimes defied Baghdad.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After internal disputes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> ended Dulafid rule in 897, reabsorbing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> into direct caliphal administration.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 825 CE – 897 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:76.12%;width:1.43%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>825 CE – 897 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karaj</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu Dulaf, Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz, Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dulafids</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=861&amp;focus=dulafid-dynasty" aria-label="Dulafid dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5967 884 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dulafid dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6287 1140L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6287 1140h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6287 1140h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Karaj. <a href="/atlas?year=861&amp;focus=dulafid-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

